[{:db/id 17592186092314 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-115e7a48-2bd1-5ad1-83e6-8c6a3be144c9" "question-3be4391a-3aa5-5bc8-9302-e0ef613d626c" "question-da692c40-031f-5c36-9d03-46c939749133" "question-a8bdf577-59f8-5a92-b08a-72c7b5bf639b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186091925 :question/problem "A store has $40$ employees, of which $21$ are female. The store is divided into three departments, $A, B,$ and $C.$ There are $7$ female workers in department $A,$ while there are $8$ female workers in department $C.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected store worker is a female that works in department $B?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that the chosen worker is a female, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected worker works department $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected worker works department $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected worker works department $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A \\sqcup B \\sqcup C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nRecall that the law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(F) = P(F \\cap A) + P(F \\cap B) + P(F \\cap C).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find $P(F \\cap B)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- To calculate $P(F \\cap A),$ we take the number of females who work in department $A$ $(7)$ and divide by the total number of workers $(40).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(F \\cap A) = \\dfrac{7}{40}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(F \\cap C),$ we take the number of females who work in department $C$ $(8)$ and divide by the total number of workers $(40).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(F \\cap C) = \\dfrac{8}{40}.\n$$\n\n- Also, we're given that\n\n$$\nP(F) = \\dfrac{21}{40}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(F \\cap B),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F) & =P(F\\cap A)+P(F\\cap B)+P(F\\cap C) \\\\ \\frac{21}{40} & =\\frac{7}{40}+P(F\\cap B)+\\frac{8}{40} \\\\ P(F\\cap B) & =\\frac{21}{40}-\\frac{7}{40}-\\frac{8}{40}. \\\\ P(F\\cap B) & =\\frac{6}{40} \\\\ P(F\\cap B) & =\\frac{3}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092315 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-296a9d4d-087e-5552-b718-aa436d5fbac8" "question-ca1787dc-fa3e-5ab1-99c5-cbfbd849d0c8" "question-7c588063-b6d3-5525-ae01-ed797a4d9cc8" "question-507f5970-478a-564c-a05d-7f723ba11f01" "question-cb95f880-a6d7-5a6d-8e15-40d3ec490c19"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186091926 :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected part is defective, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected part was produced by Factory ${A},$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected part was produced by Factory ${B},$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected part was produced by Factory ${C}.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A \\sqcup B \\sqcup C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected part is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since Factory ${A}$ produces $32\\%$ of the parts, Factory ${B}$ produces $33 \\%$ of the parts, and Factory ${C}$ produces $35 \\%$ of the parts, we have\n\n$$\nP(A) = 0.32, \\qquad P(B) = 0.33, \\qquad P(C)=0.35.\n$$\n\n- Since $1.5\\%$ of the parts produced by Factory ${A},$ $2\\%$ of the parts produced by Factory ${B},$ and $1.4\\%$ of the parts produced by Factory ${C}$ are defective, we have \n\n$$\nP(D | A) = 0.015, \\qquad P(D | B) = 0.02, \\qquad P(D | C) = 0.014.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected part is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.015)(0.32)+(0.02)(0.33)+(0.014)(0.35) \\\\  & =0.004\\,8+0.006\\,6+0.004\\,9 \\\\  & =0.016\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092316 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cad3485a-4b9b-57e5-9ec4-43f98bf6be7a" "question-ae1e8adc-b949-5f9d-889f-68a0d9d9eba2" "question-199cacb7-9e7a-5d07-af35-fc181af78213" "question-14105181-c45b-52bb-8273-83f863c9ff48"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186091927 :question/problem "Three baskets, $A, B,$ and $C,$ contain apples. It is known that $\\dfrac25$ of the apples in basket $A$ and $\\dfrac14$ of the apples in basket $B$ are red. A basket is selected at random, and an apple is randomly chosen from the basket. If the probability that the selected apple is red is $\\dfrac13,$ what proportion of the apples from basket $C$ are red?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that the selected apple is red, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected apple is from basket ${A},$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected apple is from basket ${B},$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected apple is from basket ${C}.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A \\sqcup B \\sqcup C$ covers the entire sample space. \n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a red apple, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a basket is randomly selected and there are $3$ baskets, we have\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(B) = P(C) = \\dfrac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\dfrac25$ of the apples in basket $A$ and $\\dfrac14$ of the apples in basket $B$ are red, we have\n\n$$\nP(R | A)= \\dfrac25, \\qquad P(R | B) = \\dfrac14.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a red apple from any of the three baskets is $\\dfrac13,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(R) = \\dfrac13.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(R|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =(\\frac{2}{5})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{4})(\\frac{1}{3})+P(R|C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{2}{15}+\\frac{1}{12}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}-\\frac{1}{12} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{7}{60} \\\\ P(R|C) & =\\frac{7}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{7}{20}$ of the apples from basket $C$ are red." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-115e7a48-2bd1-5ad1-83e6-8c6a3be144c9" :question/id #uuid "115e7a48-2bd1-5ad1-83e6-8c6a3be144c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-143566" :question/problem "In a toy store, a total of $80$ green and red marbles are distributed between three boxes, $A,B,$ and $C.$ Box $A$ contains $16$ red marbles, while box $B$ contains $14$ red marbles. If $50%$ of all the marbles are red, what is the probability that a marble chosen at random is a red marble from box $C?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that the selected marble is red, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected marble is from box $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected marble is from box $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected marble is from box $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nRecall that the law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(R)=P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find $P(R\\cap C):$\n\n- To calculate $P(R\\cap A),$ we take the number of red marbles in box $A$ $(16)$ and divide by the total number of marbles $(80).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(R\\cap A)=\\frac{16}{80}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(R\\cap B),$ we take the number of red marbles in box $B$ $(14)$ and divide by the total number of marbles $(80).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(R\\cap B)=\\frac{14}{80}.\n$$\n\n- Also, we're given that\n\n$$\nP(R)=50%=\\frac{40}{80}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(R\\cap C),$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\ \\frac{40}{80} & =\\frac{16}{80}+\\frac{14}{80}+P(R\\cap C) \\\\ P(R\\cap C) & =\\frac{40}{80}-\\frac{16}{80}-\\frac{14}{80} \\\\ P(R\\cap C) & =\\frac{10}{80} \\\\ P(R\\cap C) & =12.5%\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4e0424ca-fe52-5dbc-a73d-ed43b5aab248"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4e0424ca-fe52-5dbc-a73d-ed43b5aab248" :answer-field/id #uuid "4e0424ca-fe52-5dbc-a73d-ed43b5aab248" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-acf9e720-5fd0-5369-9ff8-cbfe4ad56baf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bfa09f7e-0674-5a48-82b6-905966987e9a" "answer-acf9e720-5fd0-5369-9ff8-cbfe4ad56baf" "answer-a85391e3-c2fb-5276-896b-fef0b446a7c5" "answer-888911bd-24d6-59a9-9b2b-152fff44d333" "answer-5d56c699-9c2a-5e1f-a33b-571278909975"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfa09f7e-0674-5a48-82b6-905966987e9a" :answer/id #uuid "bfa09f7e-0674-5a48-82b6-905966987e9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10.5%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-acf9e720-5fd0-5369-9ff8-cbfe4ad56baf" :answer/id #uuid "acf9e720-5fd0-5369-9ff8-cbfe4ad56baf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12.5%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a85391e3-c2fb-5276-896b-fef0b446a7c5" :answer/id #uuid "a85391e3-c2fb-5276-896b-fef0b446a7c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-888911bd-24d6-59a9-9b2b-152fff44d333" :answer/id #uuid "888911bd-24d6-59a9-9b2b-152fff44d333" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d56c699-9c2a-5e1f-a33b-571278909975" :answer/id #uuid "5d56c699-9c2a-5e1f-a33b-571278909975" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "70%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3be4391a-3aa5-5bc8-9302-e0ef613d626c" :question/id #uuid "3be4391a-3aa5-5bc8-9302-e0ef613d626c" :question/math-academy-id "q-143723" :question/problem "Among all the people that visited Bill's ice cream shop last weekend, $25%$ were females under $18,$ $20%$ were females between $18$ and $30,$ and $15%$ were females over $30.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected person who visited Bill's shop last weekend is female?" :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a person selected was a female.\n\n- let ${A}_{1}$ be the event that the person was under $18$,\n\n- let ${A}_{2}$ be the event that the person was at least  $18$ but under $30$,\n\n- let ${A}_{3}$ be the event that the person was at least $30$ or older.\n\nThen the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person was a female, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(F)=P(F\\cap {A}_{1})+P(F\\cap {A}_{2})+P(F\\cap {A}_{3})\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nP(F\\cap {A}_{1})=0.25,\\,P(F\\cap {A}_{2})=0.2\\,P(F\\cap {A}_{3})=0.15.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected person is a female is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F) & =P(F\\cap {A}_{1})+P(F\\cap {A}_{2})+P(F\\cap {A}_{3}) \\\\  & =0.25+0.2+0.15 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0ad09311-474c-5682-b225-5468fda63640"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0ad09311-474c-5682-b225-5468fda63640" :answer-field/id #uuid "0ad09311-474c-5682-b225-5468fda63640" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e24464ec-be29-5034-8294-e920ccc39cfe" :answer-field/choices ["answer-33008623-0338-5660-826c-0c65d608c914" "answer-59062f7d-4e46-50d5-8afd-8f68d15b2115" "answer-1feaa879-4a53-577a-ac18-96df41141043" "answer-e24464ec-be29-5034-8294-e920ccc39cfe" "answer-d3199c3b-4f96-548c-963e-232640c914ba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33008623-0338-5660-826c-0c65d608c914" :answer/id #uuid "33008623-0338-5660-826c-0c65d608c914" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59062f7d-4e46-50d5-8afd-8f68d15b2115" :answer/id #uuid "59062f7d-4e46-50d5-8afd-8f68d15b2115" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1feaa879-4a53-577a-ac18-96df41141043" :answer/id #uuid "1feaa879-4a53-577a-ac18-96df41141043" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.55" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e24464ec-be29-5034-8294-e920ccc39cfe" :answer/id #uuid "e24464ec-be29-5034-8294-e920ccc39cfe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3199c3b-4f96-548c-963e-232640c914ba" :answer/id #uuid "d3199c3b-4f96-548c-963e-232640c914ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092557 :question/problem "Alice has three email accounts. \nIn the first account, she received $16$ emails, of which $5$ were spam. \nIn the second account, she received $10$ emails, of which $2$ were spam. \nAnd in the third account, she received $14$ emails, of which $3$ were spam. \nBased on this data, what is the probability that a randomly selected email across all her accounts is spam?" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be the event that a chosen email is spam, and \n\n- let ${B}_{1}$ be the event that the email was received in the first account,\n\n- let ${B}_{2}$ be the event that the email was received in the second account,\n\n- let ${B}_{3}$ be the event that the email was received in the third account.\n\nThen the disjoint union ${B}_{1}⊔{B}_{2}⊔{B}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected email is spam, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S\\cap {B}_{1})+P(S\\cap {B}_{2})+P(S\\cap {B}_{3})\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that Alice received a total of \n\n$$\n16+10+14=40\n$$\n\nemails in all three accounts.\n\n- To calculate $P(S\\cap {B}_{1}),$ we take the number of emails in the first account that are spam $(5)$ and divide by the total number of emails received $(40).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap {B}_{1})=\\frac{5}{40}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(S\\cap {B}_{2}),$ we take the number of emails in the second account that are spam $(2)$ and divide by the total number of emails received $(40).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap {B}_{2})=\\frac{2}{40}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(S\\cap {B}_{3}),$ we take the number of emails in the third account that are spam $(3)$ and divide by the total number of emails received $(40).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap {B}_{3})=\\frac{3}{40}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected email is spam is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S\\cap {B}_{1})+P(S\\cap {B}_{2})+P(S\\cap {B}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{40}+\\frac{2}{40}+\\frac{3}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186089513 :answer-field/correct 17592186089516 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-da692c40-031f-5c36-9d03-46c939749133" :question/id #uuid "da692c40-031f-5c36-9d03-46c939749133" :question/math-academy-id "q-143629" :question/problem "William has a desk with three drawers, $A,$ $B,$ and $C.$ In drawer $A,$ he has $14$ markers, of which $3$ are red. In drawer $B,$ he has $18$ markers, of which $4$ are red. And, in drawer $C,$ he has $20$ markers, of which $6$ are red. If one marker is selected at random from all the markers combined, what is the probability that it is red?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that a chosen marker is red, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the marker was from drawer $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the marker was from drawer $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the marker was from drawer $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected marker is red, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(R)=P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C)\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that William has a total of \n\n$$\n14+18+20=52\n$$\n\nmarkers in all three drawers.\n\n- To calculate $P(R\\cap A),$ we take the number of markers in the first drawer that are red $(3)$ and divide by the total number of markers $(52).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(R\\cap A)=\\frac{3}{52}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(R\\cap B),$ we take the number of markers in the second drawer that are red $(4)$ and divide by the total number of markers $(52).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(R\\cap B)=\\frac{4}{52}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(R\\cap C),$ we take the number of markers in the third drawer that are red $(6)$ and divide by the total number of markers $(52).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(R\\cap C)=\\frac{6}{52}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected marker is red is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{52}+\\frac{4}{52}+\\frac{6}{52} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{52} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c78cee7c-fc01-5751-a99d-34ac02442790"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c78cee7c-fc01-5751-a99d-34ac02442790" :answer-field/id #uuid "c78cee7c-fc01-5751-a99d-34ac02442790" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5efa168a-3072-5979-9da3-04db41e094b1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f647f0e8-004c-5809-a19a-6eec1fe3e7bd" "answer-5efa168a-3072-5979-9da3-04db41e094b1" "answer-4fd45163-e185-5a85-b926-f44a23475fc2" "answer-ab4fa0d5-2bb6-57e0-8891-ccf5a806ccfa" "answer-48184edb-be98-5b30-bcb6-74e0859ae86e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f647f0e8-004c-5809-a19a-6eec1fe3e7bd" :answer/id #uuid "f647f0e8-004c-5809-a19a-6eec1fe3e7bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5efa168a-3072-5979-9da3-04db41e094b1" :answer/id #uuid "5efa168a-3072-5979-9da3-04db41e094b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4fd45163-e185-5a85-b926-f44a23475fc2" :answer/id #uuid "4fd45163-e185-5a85-b926-f44a23475fc2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab4fa0d5-2bb6-57e0-8891-ccf5a806ccfa" :answer/id #uuid "ab4fa0d5-2bb6-57e0-8891-ccf5a806ccfa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48184edb-be98-5b30-bcb6-74e0859ae86e" :answer/id #uuid "48184edb-be98-5b30-bcb6-74e0859ae86e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a8bdf577-59f8-5a92-b08a-72c7b5bf639b" :question/id #uuid "a8bdf577-59f8-5a92-b08a-72c7b5bf639b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143560" :question/problem "Among the people arrested in a particular city in 2010, $16%$ were males under $18,$ $30%$  were males between $18$ and $40,$ and $24%$ were males over $40.$ What is the probability that a person randomly selected from all those arrested in 2010 is male?" :question/worked-solution "Let $M$ be the event that a person selected was a male, and\n\n- let ${A}_{1}$ be the event that the person was under $18$,\n\n- let ${A}_{2}$ be the event that the person was between $18$ and $40$, and\n\n- let ${A}_{3}$ be the event that the person was over $40.$\n\nThen the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person is male, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(M)=P(M\\cap {A}_{1})+P(M\\cap {A}_{2})+P(M\\cap {A}_{3})\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nP(M\\cap {A}_{1})=0.16,\\,P(M\\cap {A}_{2})=0.3\\,P(M\\cap {A}_{3})=0.24.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected person is male is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M) & =P(M\\cap {A}_{1})+P(M\\cap {A}_{2})+P(M\\cap {A}_{3}) \\\\  & =0.16+0.3+0.24 \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-809d8147-8c94-5f8b-b479-0c8092abc324"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-809d8147-8c94-5f8b-b479-0c8092abc324" :answer-field/id #uuid "809d8147-8c94-5f8b-b479-0c8092abc324" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b0f14c54-9815-5d63-bc8b-4867f5bb4db3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-215b87a6-81fc-5cab-a694-78aa0095ae2d" "answer-bab1c9d3-861d-5fc5-a92c-ad4cd019e1e1" "answer-d32e28b1-e396-5963-b787-cedd10424d17" "answer-d17bdea3-83a8-52df-9f99-77630fda70c2" "answer-b0f14c54-9815-5d63-bc8b-4867f5bb4db3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-215b87a6-81fc-5cab-a694-78aa0095ae2d" :answer/id #uuid "215b87a6-81fc-5cab-a694-78aa0095ae2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.29" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bab1c9d3-861d-5fc5-a92c-ad4cd019e1e1" :answer/id #uuid "bab1c9d3-861d-5fc5-a92c-ad4cd019e1e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d32e28b1-e396-5963-b787-cedd10424d17" :answer/id #uuid "d32e28b1-e396-5963-b787-cedd10424d17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d17bdea3-83a8-52df-9f99-77630fda70c2" :answer/id #uuid "d17bdea3-83a8-52df-9f99-77630fda70c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.65" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0f14c54-9815-5d63-bc8b-4867f5bb4db3" :answer/id #uuid "b0f14c54-9815-5d63-bc8b-4867f5bb4db3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-296a9d4d-087e-5552-b718-aa436d5fbac8" :question/id #uuid "296a9d4d-087e-5552-b718-aa436d5fbac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143748" :question/problem "A company produces replacement batteries for a particular cell phone model at three locations. Location $A$ produces $30%$ of the batteries, of which $3%$ are defective. Location $B$ produces $38%$ of the batteries, of which $5%$ are defective. And location $C$  produces $32%$ of the batteries, of which also $5%$ are defective. What is the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that the battery is defective, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the battery was produced at location $A,$\n\n- let $B$  be the event that the battery was produced at location $B,$\n\n- let $C$  be the event that the battery was produced at location $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since  location $A$ produces $30%$ of the batteries,  location $B$ produces $38%$ of the batteries, and location $C$ produces $32%$ of the batteries, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.3,\\,P(B)=0.38,\\,P(C)=0.32.\n$$\n\n- Since $3%$ of the batteries produced at location $A$, $5%$ of the batteries produced at location $B$, and $5%$ of the batteries produced at  location $C,$ are defective, we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.03,\\,P(D|B)=0.05,\\,P(D|C)=0.05.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.03)(0.3)+(0.05)(0.38)+(0.05)(0.32) \\\\  & =0.009+0.019+0.016 \\\\  & =0.044.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-efcaebab-808b-586a-b686-8d0602967092"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-efcaebab-808b-586a-b686-8d0602967092" :answer-field/id #uuid "efcaebab-808b-586a-b686-8d0602967092" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-63016c40-26b8-54f1-af02-fe4df536e028" :answer-field/choices ["answer-63016c40-26b8-54f1-af02-fe4df536e028" "answer-efcedd14-6e3b-5303-8273-c5f15c5397fe" "answer-e6bab3ee-bcc0-51a4-967f-ae1ffb30e505" "answer-9e6154ce-f843-5fdc-a8b0-5dfb33599b4e" "answer-cc67574e-605f-521d-93f0-a43b7eb005d7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63016c40-26b8-54f1-af02-fe4df536e028" :answer/id #uuid "63016c40-26b8-54f1-af02-fe4df536e028" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.044" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-efcedd14-6e3b-5303-8273-c5f15c5397fe" :answer/id #uuid "efcedd14-6e3b-5303-8273-c5f15c5397fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.054" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6bab3ee-bcc0-51a4-967f-ae1ffb30e505" :answer/id #uuid "e6bab3ee-bcc0-51a4-967f-ae1ffb30e505" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.056" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e6154ce-f843-5fdc-a8b0-5dfb33599b4e" :answer/id #uuid "9e6154ce-f843-5fdc-a8b0-5dfb33599b4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.058" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc67574e-605f-521d-93f0-a43b7eb005d7" :answer/id #uuid "cc67574e-605f-521d-93f0-a43b7eb005d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.541" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ca1787dc-fa3e-5ab1-99c5-cbfbd849d0c8" :question/id #uuid "ca1787dc-fa3e-5ab1-99c5-cbfbd849d0c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143747" :question/problem "Three bags contain marbles. Bag $1$ contains $12$ red marbles and $8$ green marbles. Bag $2$ contains $14$ red marbles and $6$ green marbles. And bag $3$ contains $10$ red marbles and $10$ green marbles. A bag is selected at random, and a marble is randomly selected from the bag. What is the probability that the marble is red?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event of selecting a red marble at random from one of the three bags, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the marble is from bag $1,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the marble is from bag $2,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the marble is from bag $3.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a red marble at random from one of the three bags, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a bag is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $12$ red marbles out of $20$ in bag $1,$ $14$ red marbles out of $20$ in bag $2,$ and $10$ red marbles out of $20$  in bag $3,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(R|A)=\\frac{12}{20},\\,P(R|B)=\\frac{14}{20},\\,P(R|C)=\\frac{10}{20}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability of selecting a red marble at random from one of the three bags is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(\\frac{12}{20})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{14}{20})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{10}{20})(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{60}+\\frac{14}{60}+\\frac{10}{60} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{60} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-85fd5ffd-e434-5aa7-93f5-7f4265ffa862"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-85fd5ffd-e434-5aa7-93f5-7f4265ffa862" :answer-field/id #uuid "85fd5ffd-e434-5aa7-93f5-7f4265ffa862" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-05abde05-5ea0-5346-b70b-e15ddb38a2b0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-60c59049-514f-5905-b9eb-4d7a362f5ce6" "answer-233072a2-5bde-566b-9863-22d8af3afca9" "answer-18474b78-45ed-57a3-8328-b554a713c479" "answer-5c42dd93-109c-5ae0-8799-e0b629f2949b" "answer-05abde05-5ea0-5346-b70b-e15ddb38a2b0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60c59049-514f-5905-b9eb-4d7a362f5ce6" :answer/id #uuid "60c59049-514f-5905-b9eb-4d7a362f5ce6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-233072a2-5bde-566b-9863-22d8af3afca9" :answer/id #uuid "233072a2-5bde-566b-9863-22d8af3afca9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18474b78-45ed-57a3-8328-b554a713c479" :answer/id #uuid "18474b78-45ed-57a3-8328-b554a713c479" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c42dd93-109c-5ae0-8799-e0b629f2949b" :answer/id #uuid "5c42dd93-109c-5ae0-8799-e0b629f2949b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05abde05-5ea0-5346-b70b-e15ddb38a2b0" :answer/id #uuid "05abde05-5ea0-5346-b70b-e15ddb38a2b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7c588063-b6d3-5525-ae01-ed797a4d9cc8" :question/id #uuid "7c588063-b6d3-5525-ae01-ed797a4d9cc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143753" :question/problem "In a gas station, $40%$ of the clients buy Regular gasoline, $45%$ buy Premium gasoline, and $15%$ buy Supreme gasoline. If $65%$ of the clients that purchase Regular gasoline paid with a credit card, $50%$ of the clients that buy Premium gasoline paid with a credit card, and $60%$ of the clients that buy Supreme gasoline paid with a credit card,  what is the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card?" :question/worked-solution "Let  $C$ be the event that the client paid with a credit card, and\n\n- let $R$ be the event that the client bought Regular gasoline,\n\n- let $Q$  be the event that the client bought Premium gasoline,\n\n- let $S$ be the event that the client bought Supreme gasoline.\n\nThen the disjoint union $R⊔Q⊔S$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C\\cap R)+P(C\\cap Q)+P(C\\cap S) \\\\  & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|Q)P(Q)+P(C|S)P(S)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40%$ of the clients buy Regular gasoline, $45%$ of the clients buy Premium gasoline, and $15%$ of the clients buy Supreme gasoline, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.4,\\,P(Q)=0.45,\\,P(S)=0.15.\n$$\n\n- Since $65%$ of the clients that buy Regular gasoline, $50%$ of the clients that buy Premium gasoline, and $60%$ of the clients that buy Supreme gasoline,  paid with a credit card, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|R)=0.65,\\,P(C|Q)=0.5,\\,P(C|S)=0.6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|Q)P(Q)+P(C|S)P(S) \\\\  & =(0.65)(0.4)+(0.5)(0.45)+(0.6)(0.15) \\\\  & =0.26+0.225+0.009 \\\\  & =0.575.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e132532a-be97-5775-bb17-2406d8caeef2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e132532a-be97-5775-bb17-2406d8caeef2" :answer-field/id #uuid "e132532a-be97-5775-bb17-2406d8caeef2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-82363ae0-ea2c-5ea2-bd36-f1a97cf797bb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-32133a22-82bc-5a7c-9eac-727b8484e3b4" "answer-14a66e4d-d36b-5589-9e76-8d2fc1124283" "answer-357f442f-a27d-5c2b-8c03-4173f9ed1135" "answer-82363ae0-ea2c-5ea2-bd36-f1a97cf797bb" "answer-35872c71-32ca-5629-8a73-9868fcb5d5a8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32133a22-82bc-5a7c-9eac-727b8484e3b4" :answer/id #uuid "32133a22-82bc-5a7c-9eac-727b8484e3b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.515" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14a66e4d-d36b-5589-9e76-8d2fc1124283" :answer/id #uuid "14a66e4d-d36b-5589-9e76-8d2fc1124283" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.525" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-357f442f-a27d-5c2b-8c03-4173f9ed1135" :answer/id #uuid "357f442f-a27d-5c2b-8c03-4173f9ed1135" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.555" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82363ae0-ea2c-5ea2-bd36-f1a97cf797bb" :answer/id #uuid "82363ae0-ea2c-5ea2-bd36-f1a97cf797bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.575" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35872c71-32ca-5629-8a73-9868fcb5d5a8" :answer/id #uuid "35872c71-32ca-5629-8a73-9868fcb5d5a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.625" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-507f5970-478a-564c-a05d-7f723ba11f01" :question/id #uuid "507f5970-478a-564c-a05d-7f723ba11f01" :question/math-academy-id "q-143626" :question/problem "A company manufactures three types of cars. Of the car population, $40%$ are model ${C}_{1},$  $35%$ are model ${C}_{2},$ and the remaining cars are model ${C}_{3}.$ If $6%$ of the model ${C}_{1},$ $4%$ of the model  ${C}_{2},$ and $2%$ of the model ${C}_{3}$ cars break down during the first year, what is the probability that a randomly selected car will break down during the first year?" :question/worked-solution "Let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected car breaks down during the first year, and \n\n- let ${C}_{1}$ be the event that the car is of model ${C}_{1},$\n\n- let ${C}_{2}$ be the event that the car is of model ${C}_{2},$\n\n- let ${C}_{3}$ be the event that the car is of model ${C}_{3}.$\n\nThen the disjoint union ${C}_{1}⊔{C}_{2}⊔{C}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected car will break down during the first year, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B) & =P(B\\cap {C}_{1})+P(B\\cap {C}_{2})+P(B\\cap {C}_{3}) \\\\  & =P(B|{C}_{1})P({C}_{1})+P(B|{C}_{2})P({C}_{2})+P(B|{C}_{3})P({C}_{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40%$ of the cars produced by the company are model ${C}_{1},$ and $50%$ are model ${C}_{2},$  the model ${C}_{3}$ cars make up the remaining\n\n$$\n100%-(40%+35%)=25%.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nP({C}_{1})=0.4,\\,P({C}_{2})=0.35,\\,P({C}_{3})=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $6%$ of the cars of model ${C}_{1},$ $4%$ of those of model  ${C}_{2},$ and $2%$ of the cars of model ${C}_{3}$ break down during the first year, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|{C}_{1})=0.06,\\,P(B|{C}_{2})=0.04,\\,P(B|{C}_{3})=0.02.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected car breaks down during the first year is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B) & =P(B|{C}_{1})P({C}_{1})+P(B|{C}_{2})P({C}_{2})+P(B|{C}_{3})P({C}_{3}) \\\\  & =(0.06)(0.4)+(0.04)(0.35)+(0.02)(0.25) \\\\  & =0.024+0.014+0.005 \\\\  & =0.043.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e7e78d0f-3ddc-5e29-ac7f-d4654fbb2dea"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e7e78d0f-3ddc-5e29-ac7f-d4654fbb2dea" :answer-field/id #uuid "e7e78d0f-3ddc-5e29-ac7f-d4654fbb2dea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a2068431-9034-514f-adff-ba0037c20a7b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-306c6c6b-a3f0-535f-b65f-c01df8a0c6a4" "answer-a2068431-9034-514f-adff-ba0037c20a7b" "answer-706804b2-6f19-5053-8f45-588b0942a4eb" "answer-315b5ce4-cdef-5a05-900d-d09c2a07e7be" "answer-95bd2b23-5128-5cab-b41d-ef4058310976"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-306c6c6b-a3f0-535f-b65f-c01df8a0c6a4" :answer/id #uuid "306c6c6b-a3f0-535f-b65f-c01df8a0c6a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.041" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2068431-9034-514f-adff-ba0037c20a7b" :answer/id #uuid "a2068431-9034-514f-adff-ba0037c20a7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.043" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-706804b2-6f19-5053-8f45-588b0942a4eb" :answer/id #uuid "706804b2-6f19-5053-8f45-588b0942a4eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.051" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-315b5ce4-cdef-5a05-900d-d09c2a07e7be" :answer/id #uuid "315b5ce4-cdef-5a05-900d-d09c2a07e7be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.342" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95bd2b23-5128-5cab-b41d-ef4058310976" :answer/id #uuid "95bd2b23-5128-5cab-b41d-ef4058310976" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.65" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cb95f880-a6d7-5a6d-8e15-40d3ec490c19" :question/id #uuid "cb95f880-a6d7-5a6d-8e15-40d3ec490c19" :question/math-academy-id "q-143749" :question/problem "Three companies supply widgets to a car shop. Company $A$ supplies $45%$ of the widgets, of which $2%$ are defective. Company $B$ provides $40%$ of the widgets, of which  $3%$ are defective. And company $C$ supplies the remaining widgets, of which $4%$ are defective. What is the probability that a randomly chosen widget from these companies will turn out to be defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that the widget is defective, and  \n\n- let $A$ be that the event that the widget was produced by company $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be that the event  that the widget was produced by company $B$,\n\n- let $C$ be that the event that the widget was produced by company $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.  \n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly  selected widget  is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since Company $A$ supplies $45%$ and Company $B$ produces $40%$ of the widgets, then Company $B$ produces the remaining\n\n$$\n100%-(45%+40%)=15%.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.45,\\,P(B)=0.4,\\,P(C)=0.15.\n$$\n\n- Since $2%$ of the widgets produced by Company $A$, $3%$ of the widgets produced by Company $B$, and $4%$ of the widgets produced by Company $C,$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.02,\\,P(D|B)=0.03,\\,P(D|C)=0.04.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected widget turns out to be defective is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.02)(0.45)+(0.03)(0.4)+(0.04)(0.15) \\\\  & =0.009+0.012+0.006 \\\\  & =0.027.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c21336b1-0625-5280-8d3b-20f566b7feef"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c21336b1-0625-5280-8d3b-20f566b7feef" :answer-field/id #uuid "c21336b1-0625-5280-8d3b-20f566b7feef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-357f0b32-a56b-52d7-95ec-1d35f74b7cc1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-357f0b32-a56b-52d7-95ec-1d35f74b7cc1" "answer-60025f2b-4528-5cf1-84f8-269bccbd14fd" "answer-30da36d7-24a3-55da-be75-a03f2fba54fb" "answer-573e601d-0da3-5909-a0c9-3846969898b1" "answer-f9341cb2-5d0e-586a-890f-9d8f2bd36dd7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-357f0b32-a56b-52d7-95ec-1d35f74b7cc1" :answer/id #uuid "357f0b32-a56b-52d7-95ec-1d35f74b7cc1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.027" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60025f2b-4528-5cf1-84f8-269bccbd14fd" :answer/id #uuid "60025f2b-4528-5cf1-84f8-269bccbd14fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.085" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-30da36d7-24a3-55da-be75-a03f2fba54fb" :answer/id #uuid "30da36d7-24a3-55da-be75-a03f2fba54fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.152" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-573e601d-0da3-5909-a0c9-3846969898b1" :answer/id #uuid "573e601d-0da3-5909-a0c9-3846969898b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.211" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9341cb2-5d0e-586a-890f-9d8f2bd36dd7" :answer/id #uuid "f9341cb2-5d0e-586a-890f-9d8f2bd36dd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.421" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cad3485a-4b9b-57e5-9ec4-43f98bf6be7a" :question/id #uuid "cad3485a-4b9b-57e5-9ec4-43f98bf6be7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143877" :question/problem "A company manufactures three types of cars. Of the total car population, $40%$ are model ${M}_{1},$ $35%$ are model ${M}_{2},$ and $25%$ are model ${M}_{3}.$ If $6%$ of model ${M}_{1}$ and $4%$ of model ${M}_{2}$ cars break down during the first year, and the probability that a randomly selected car from any of the three models breaks down during the first year is $0.045,$ what percentage of model ${M}_{3}$ cars will break down during the first year?" :question/worked-solution "Let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected car breaks down during the first year, and \n\n- let ${M}_{1}$ be the event that the car is model ${M}_{1},$\n\n- let ${M}_{2}$ be the event that the car is model ${M}_{2},$\n\n- let ${M}_{3}$ be the event that the car is model ${M}_{3}.$\n\nThen the disjoint union ${M}_{1}⊔{M}_{2}⊔{M}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected car will break down during the first year, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B) & =P(B\\cap {M}_{1})+P(B\\cap {M}_{2})+P(B\\cap {M}_{3}) \\\\  & =P(B|{M}_{1})P({M}_{1})+P(B|{M}_{2})P({M}_{2})+P(B|{M}_{3})P({M}_{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40%$ of cars are model ${M}_{1},$, $35%$ are model ${M}_{2}$, and $25%$ are model ${M}_{3},$ we have\n\n$$\nP({M}_{1})=0.4,\\,P({M}_{2})=0.35,\\,P({M}_{3})=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $6%$ of model ${M}_{1}$ and $4%$ of model  ${M}_{2}$ break down during the first year, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|{M}_{1})=0.06,\\,P(B|{M}_{2})=0.04.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected car breaks down during the first year is $0.045,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.045.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(B|{M}_{3}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B) & =P(B|{M}_{1})P({M}_{1})+P(B|{M}_{2})P({M}_{2})+P(B|{M}_{3})P({M}_{3}) \\\\ 0.045 & =(0.06)(0.4)+(0.04)(0.35)+P(B|{M}_{3})(0.25) \\\\ 0.045 & =0.024+0.014+0.25P(B|{M}_{3}) \\\\ 0.25P(B|{M}_{3}) & =0.007 \\\\ P(B|{M}_{3}) & =0.028=2.8%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2.8%$ of model ${M}_{3}$ cars break down during the first year." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6b8dcb20-7e9f-5dac-bfdc-4def0a6201e0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6b8dcb20-7e9f-5dac-bfdc-4def0a6201e0" :answer-field/id #uuid "6b8dcb20-7e9f-5dac-bfdc-4def0a6201e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-93d317ba-d06f-53ee-9594-becc9ecb5ea2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9a8da790-3e42-5a7f-b4b2-a5fd942dcb65" "answer-6a530138-b638-5639-a711-718842186f49" "answer-93d317ba-d06f-53ee-9594-becc9ecb5ea2" "answer-488a3705-98d3-5a24-9004-41ab9c1c321c" "answer-87ddb4d4-dca4-56d7-900e-03e65bd00020"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a8da790-3e42-5a7f-b4b2-a5fd942dcb65" :answer/id #uuid "9a8da790-3e42-5a7f-b4b2-a5fd942dcb65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.4%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a530138-b638-5639-a711-718842186f49" :answer/id #uuid "6a530138-b638-5639-a711-718842186f49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.6%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93d317ba-d06f-53ee-9594-becc9ecb5ea2" :answer/id #uuid "93d317ba-d06f-53ee-9594-becc9ecb5ea2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.8%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-488a3705-98d3-5a24-9004-41ab9c1c321c" :answer/id #uuid "488a3705-98d3-5a24-9004-41ab9c1c321c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.2%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87ddb4d4-dca4-56d7-900e-03e65bd00020" :answer/id #uuid "87ddb4d4-dca4-56d7-900e-03e65bd00020" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.6%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ae1e8adc-b949-5f9d-889f-68a0d9d9eba2" :question/id #uuid "ae1e8adc-b949-5f9d-889f-68a0d9d9eba2" :question/math-academy-id "q-144013" :question/problem "Three similar bags, $A,\\,B$, and $C,$ contain balls. Bag $A$ contains $4$ red and $6$ green balls, and bag $B$ contains $6$ red and $4$ green balls. A bag is selected at random, and a ball is randomly selected from the bag. If the probability that the selected ball is green is $\\frac{3}{5},$ what proportion of balls from bag $C$ are green?" :question/worked-solution "Let $G$ be the event of selecting a green ball at random from one of the three bags, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the ball is from bag $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the ball is from $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the ball is from bag $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a green ball, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G\\cap A)+P(G\\cap B)+P(G\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|B)P(B)+P(G|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a bag is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $6$ green balls out of $10$ in bag $A,$ and $4$ green balls out of $10$ in bag $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=\\frac{6}{10},\\,P(G|B)=\\frac{4}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of choosing a green ball is $\\frac{3}{5},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G)=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(G|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|B)P(B)+P(G|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & =(\\frac{6}{10})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{4}{10})(\\frac{1}{3})+P(G|C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{15}+(\\frac{1}{3})P(G|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(G|C) & =\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{2}{15} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(G|C) & =\\frac{4}{15} \\\\ P(G|C) & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{4}{5}$ of the balls in bag $C$ are green." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5dda8d7f-6956-55ad-9209-2cf83e277760"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5dda8d7f-6956-55ad-9209-2cf83e277760" :answer-field/id #uuid "5dda8d7f-6956-55ad-9209-2cf83e277760" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3a2bfeb5-10a6-5364-95db-232f11580849" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b3684175-dfef-564c-9015-7ac3c04daa84" "answer-cc71899e-2b08-5805-bb87-08237cd8b8dc" "answer-534d1982-5794-5ef3-b6c7-550fa347ae5a" "answer-3a2bfeb5-10a6-5364-95db-232f11580849" "answer-e29dcf7e-fe90-5acc-b60e-5b9007f23b8e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3684175-dfef-564c-9015-7ac3c04daa84" :answer/id #uuid "b3684175-dfef-564c-9015-7ac3c04daa84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc71899e-2b08-5805-bb87-08237cd8b8dc" :answer/id #uuid "cc71899e-2b08-5805-bb87-08237cd8b8dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-534d1982-5794-5ef3-b6c7-550fa347ae5a" :answer/id #uuid "534d1982-5794-5ef3-b6c7-550fa347ae5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a2bfeb5-10a6-5364-95db-232f11580849" :answer/id #uuid "3a2bfeb5-10a6-5364-95db-232f11580849" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e29dcf7e-fe90-5acc-b60e-5b9007f23b8e" :answer/id #uuid "e29dcf7e-fe90-5acc-b60e-5b9007f23b8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-199cacb7-9e7a-5d07-af35-fc181af78213" :question/id #uuid "199cacb7-9e7a-5d07-af35-fc181af78213" :question/math-academy-id "q-143872" :question/problem "A company manufactures electronic components. Of the component population, $45%$ are produced by machine $A,$  $25%$ by machine $B,$ and $30%$ by machine $C.$ If $5%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ and $3%$ of those produced by machine $B$ are defective, and the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is $0.042,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $C$ is defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected component is defective, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation. \n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $45%$ of the components, machine $B$ produces $25%$ of the components, and machine $C$ produces $30%$ of the components, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.45,\\,P(B)=0.25,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $5%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ and $3%$ of the components produced by machine $B$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.05,\\,P(D|B)=0.03\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is $0.042,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.042.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.042 & =(0.05)(0.45)+(0.03)(0.25)+P(D|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.042 & =0.0225+0.0075+0.3P(D|C) \\\\ 0.3P(D|C) & =0.012 \\\\ P(D|C) & =0.04\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $C$ is defective is $0.04.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e674252e-02a4-53ae-adde-553bbd0c47c9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e674252e-02a4-53ae-adde-553bbd0c47c9" :answer-field/id #uuid "e674252e-02a4-53ae-adde-553bbd0c47c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ea852479-a868-5154-ba52-8c97031f5080" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e4fcf1de-ebb1-592d-9f04-b9c050c3551b" "answer-9482cc37-4f52-55f0-820b-76e7d8d26527" "answer-ea852479-a868-5154-ba52-8c97031f5080" "answer-49d67ba6-1b1f-58fa-a1c0-e61e944c3560" "answer-82276f72-0877-5798-8fe1-76ed94a97b68"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4fcf1de-ebb1-592d-9f04-b9c050c3551b" :answer/id #uuid "e4fcf1de-ebb1-592d-9f04-b9c050c3551b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0052" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9482cc37-4f52-55f0-820b-76e7d8d26527" :answer/id #uuid "9482cc37-4f52-55f0-820b-76e7d8d26527" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.038" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea852479-a868-5154-ba52-8c97031f5080" :answer/id #uuid "ea852479-a868-5154-ba52-8c97031f5080" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.04" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49d67ba6-1b1f-58fa-a1c0-e61e944c3560" :answer/id #uuid "49d67ba6-1b1f-58fa-a1c0-e61e944c3560" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.044" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82276f72-0877-5798-8fe1-76ed94a97b68" :answer/id #uuid "82276f72-0877-5798-8fe1-76ed94a97b68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.045" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092563 :question/problem "At a gas station, $40%$ of the customers buy Regular gasoline, $45%$ buy Premium gasoline, and $15%$ buy Supreme gasoline. It is known that $65%$ of the customers who purchase Regular gasoline and $50%$ of the customers who buy Premium gasoline pay with a credit card. If the probability that a randomly selected customer pays with a credit card is $57.5%,$ what percentage of Supreme gasoline customers pay using a credit card?" :question/worked-solution "Let  $C$ be the event that the selected person pays using a credit card, and\n\n- let $R$ be the event that the customer bought Regular gasoline,\n\n- let $Q$  be the event that the customer bought Premium gasoline,\n\n- let $S$ be the event that the customer bought Supreme gasoline.\n\nThen the disjoint union $R⊔Q⊔S$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected customer pays using a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C\\cap R)+P(C\\cap Q)+P(C\\cap S) \\\\  & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|Q)P(Q)+P(C|S)P(S)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40%$ of the customers buy Regular gasoline, $45%$ of the customers buy Premium gasoline, and $15%$ of the customers buy Supreme gasoline, we have \n\n$$\nP(R)=0.4,\\,P(Q)=0.45,\\,P(S)=0.15.\n$$\n\n- Since $65%$ of the customers that buy Regular gasoline and  $50%$ of the customers that buy Premium gasoline pay with a credit card, we have \n\n$$\nP(C|R)=0.65,\\,P(C|Q)=0.5\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected customer pays with a credit card is $57.5%,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(C)=0.575.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(C|S),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|Q)P(Q)+P(C|S)P(S) \\\\ 0.575 & =(0.65)(0.4)+(0.5)(0.45)+P(C|S)(0.15) \\\\ 0.575 & =0.26+0.225+0.15P(C|S) \\\\ 0.15P(C|S) & =0.09 \\\\ P(C|S) & =0.6=60%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $60%$ of Supreme gasoline customers pay with a credit card." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186089549 :answer-field/correct 17592186089551 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-14105181-c45b-52bb-8273-83f863c9ff48" :question/id #uuid "14105181-c45b-52bb-8273-83f863c9ff48" :question/math-academy-id "q-143995" :question/problem "A company manufactures NY Yankees caps at three different plants. Plant $A$ produces $36%$ of the caps, of which $25%$ are white, Plant $B$ produces $34%$ of the caps, of which  $40%$ are white, and Plant $C$ produces the remaining $30%$ of all the caps. If the probability that a randomly selected cap from any of the three plants is white is $0.46,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected cap from plant $C$ is white?" :question/worked-solution "Let $W$ be the event that a randomly selected cap is white, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the cap is from plant $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the cap is from plant $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the cap is from plant $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected cap from any of the three plants is white, we can use the law of total probability:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =P(W\\cap A)+P(W\\cap B)+P(W\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|B)P(B)+P(W|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since plant $A$ produces $36%$ of all the caps, plant $B$ produces $34%$ of all the caps, and plant $C$ produces $30%$ of all the caps, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.36,\\,P(B)=0.34,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $25%$ of the caps from plant $A$ and $40%$ of the caps from plant $B$ are white, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|A)=0.25,\\,P(W|B)=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected cap from any of the three plants is white is $0.46,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(W)=0.46.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(W|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|B)P(B)+P(W|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.46 & =(0.25)(0.36)+(0.4)(0.34)+P(W|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.46 & =0.09+0.136+0.3P(W|C) \\\\ 0.3P(W|C) & =0.234 \\\\ P(W|C) & =0.78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected cap from plant $C$ is white is $0.78.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6631a042-bc4e-5a30-993a-12ef7f46bef3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6631a042-bc4e-5a30-993a-12ef7f46bef3" :answer-field/id #uuid "6631a042-bc4e-5a30-993a-12ef7f46bef3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3574dea6-9246-5cae-a2cf-9c3e11c20d81" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0af764f3-b723-5507-8469-f93dbd86be3a" "answer-cce469ac-4cda-56d9-8c5a-051399a581af" "answer-bbd58733-0f63-54ef-9f6c-dd8dcfdc1bbd" "answer-3574dea6-9246-5cae-a2cf-9c3e11c20d81" "answer-6aa53c70-acee-5628-a03a-9893e9d3f000"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0af764f3-b723-5507-8469-f93dbd86be3a" :answer/id #uuid "0af764f3-b723-5507-8469-f93dbd86be3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.27" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cce469ac-4cda-56d9-8c5a-051399a581af" :answer/id #uuid "cce469ac-4cda-56d9-8c5a-051399a581af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbd58733-0f63-54ef-9f6c-dd8dcfdc1bbd" :answer/id #uuid "bbd58733-0f63-54ef-9f6c-dd8dcfdc1bbd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.54" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3574dea6-9246-5cae-a2cf-9c3e11c20d81" :answer/id #uuid "3574dea6-9246-5cae-a2cf-9c3e11c20d81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.78" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6aa53c70-acee-5628-a03a-9893e9d3f000" :answer/id #uuid "6aa53c70-acee-5628-a03a-9893e9d3f000" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.82" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093475 :tutorial/content "Suppose that three events, $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3,$ exist within a sample space $\\mathcal S$ such that \n\n- $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3$ are *mutually exclusive*, and\n\n- the events $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3$ cover the *entire* sample space.\n\nWhen these two conditions are satisfied, we say that $\\mathcal S$ is the **disjoint union** of  $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3,$ and we write \n \n\n$$\n\\mathcal S = B_1 \\sqcup B_2 \\sqcup B_3.\n$$\n\nA diagram of this situation is shown below. Notice that $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3$ cover the entire diagram but do not overlap.\n\n![](images/f5/f5f9f04eb6affb9dca1f7d4e7d66d76c4d520cbf66db93db60639ce896fb19af.png)\n\n \n\nNow suppose that we wish to compute the probability of an event $A$ that lies within $\\mathcal S.$ Let's draw this event on our diagram and label its intersections with $B_1, B_2, B_3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ed/ed6568369a3af39cf1f6ecd5cb481de20d6c31bbce0fa1be54eb9ae0ab4e7f20.png)\n\n \n\nThe **law of total probability** can be deduced from this diagram. It states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A\\cap {B}_{1})+P(A\\cap {B}_{2})+P(A\\cap {B}_{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will only consider a disjoint union of $\\mathcal S$ consisting of three events. However, it should be noted that the law of total probability can be extended to an arbitrary number of events, that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A\\cap {B}_{1})+P(A\\cap {B}_{2})+⋯ \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}P(A\\cap {B}_{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathcal S = B_1 \\sqcup B_2\\sqcup \\ldots$ is a disjoint union of $\\mathcal S.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093477 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a particular garden is divided into three small plots. There are $10$ roses in the first plot, of which $5$ are red, $12$ roses in the second plot, of which $6$ are red, and $14$ roses in the third plot, of which $4$ are red. What is the probability that a randomly selected rose from the garden is red?\n\nLet $A$ be the event that a chosen rose is red, and \n\n- let $B_1$ be the event that the selected rose comes from the first plot,\n\n- let $B_2$ be the event that the selected rose comes from the second plot,\n\n- let $B_3$ be the event that the selected rose comes from the third plot.\n\nNotice that the disjoint union\n\n$$\nB_1\\sqcup B_2\\sqcup B_3\n$$\n\ncovers the entire sample space, and that there are a total of \n\n$$\n10+12+14=36\n$$\n\nroses in the garden. \n\n- To calculate $P(A \\cap B_1),$ we take the number of red roses in the first plot $(5)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(36).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(A \\cap B_1) = \\dfrac{5}{36}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(A \\cap B_2),$ we take the number of red roses in the second plot $(6)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(36).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(A \\cap B_2) = \\dfrac{6}{36}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(A \\cap B_3),$ we take the number of red roses in the third plot $(4)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(36).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(A \\cap B_3) = \\dfrac{4}{36}.\n$$\n\nSo, to calculate the probability that a randomly chosen rose is red, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A\\cap {B}_{1})+P(A\\cap {B}_{2})+P(A\\cap {B}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{36}+\\frac{6}{36}+\\frac{4}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNote the following: \n\n- You may have noticed that there is an easier way to solve this problem. That is, to sum the total number of red roses $(5+6+4 = 15)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(10+12+14 = 36).$ However, you're encouraged to work with the law of total probability as it's essential to get used to the concept.\n\n- A little later in this lesson, we'll work with a version of the law of total probability that isn't intuitive. Understanding the law of total probability in its simplest form will help when things get trickier.\n\nLet's see another example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093476 :tutorial/content "The law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(A\\cap B_1) +  P(A\\cap B_2) +  P(A\\cap B_3),\n$$\n\nwhere $B_1\\sqcup B_2\\sqcup B_3$ is a disjoint union of the sample space $\\mathcal S.$\n\nOften, it's more helpful to express this law using conditional probabilities. \n\nFrom the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cap {B}_{1}) & =P(A|{B}_{1})P({B}_{1}) \\\\ P(A\\cap {B}_{2}) & =P(A|{B}_{2})P({B}_{2}) \\\\ P(A\\cap {B}_{3}) & =P(A|{B}_{3})P({B}_{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above form into the law of total probability, we get\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(A|B_1)P(B_1)+  P(A|B_2)P(B_2) +  P(A|B_3)P(B_3).\n$$\n\nLet's see an example of how to apply this." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092453 :activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093201 :step/math-academy-id 836329 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093206 :step/math-academy-id 836330 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093202 :step/math-academy-id 836331 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093203 :step/math-academy-id 836334 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093204 :step/math-academy-id 836335 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093205 :step/math-academy-id 836338 :db/ensure [:step/validate]}]
